{
    "title": "Addressing Security Risks From Price Fixing and Anti-Competitive Behavior in the Food Supply Chain",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 10, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-10/pdf/2025-22537.pdf",
    "summary": "The White House has issued an order to crack down on price fixing and other unfair practices in the food supply chain, with a special focus on companies controlled from outside the United States. The goal is to protect the affordability and security of the nation\u2019s food supply.\n\nTwo new task forces\u2014one at the Department of Justice and one at the Federal Trade Commission\u2014will investigate whether companies in areas like meat processing, seeds, fertilizer, and farm equipment are restricting competition or driving up prices. If they find wrongdoing, they can bring cases, suggest new rules, and, in criminal cases involving collusion, pursue prosecutions. The agencies will brief congressional leaders on their progress at the six\u2011month and one\u2011year marks, while keeping details of ongoing cases confidential.\n\nConsumers stand to benefit if the effort leads to lower or more stable food prices and a fairer marketplace. Independent farmers, ranchers, small processors, and honest competitors could also gain if the playing field is leveled and powerful firms face more scrutiny. Supporters will see this as a push to strengthen both household budgets and national security by ensuring a resilient food system.\n\nOn the other hand, large firms\u2014especially those with foreign owners\u2014may face greater oversight, legal risks, and higher compliance costs. Some businesses could see more uncertainty or be required to change common industry practices. There\u2019s also a chance that aggressive enforcement could have unintended effects, such as slowing investment or disrupting established supply relationships, depending on how investigations unfold.\n\nThe order does not change existing laws, create new rights for private lawsuits, or guarantee specific outcomes; it directs agencies to use their current powers and resources, subject to available funding. In short, this is a signal that federal watchdogs will take a closer look at how food reaches American tables\u2014and whether the system is fair to both consumers and competitors.",
